[The two matched solutions and their forbidden forms] Let $w_{\mathrm{dec}}$ and $w_{\mathrm{grow}}$ be the solutions of the transformed equation with terminal Airy data $(A_{h},A_{h}')$ and $(B_{h},B_{h}')$ at the right endpoint $\zeta(s_{0})=\zeta(\xi_{0}+s_{0})$, and put $u_{\mathrm{dec}}=a\, w_{\mathrm{dec}}\circ\zeta$, $u_{\mathrm{grow}}=a\,w_{\mathrm{grow}}\circ \zeta$; these are exact solutions of the model equation, and they are linearly independent because the terminal Wronskian is $A_{h}B_{h}'-A_{h}'B_{h} =h^{-2/3}\pi^{-1}
e0$. By [F3] the differences $w_{\mathrm{dec}}-A_{h}$ and $w_{\mathrm{grow}}-B_{h}$ are bounded by the value envelope times $(e^{\varepsilon_{h}}-1)$ and their derivatives by the derivative envelope times the same factor, with $\varepsilon_{h}\le C_{1}h$. On the forbidden side $\xi>\xi_{0}$ one has $\zeta>0$, and [F2] gives $A_{h}(\zeta)=	frac12\pi^{-1/2}(\zeta/h^{2/3})^{-1/4} e^{-\Sigma(\xi)}(1+O(h))$ and $B_{h}(\zeta)=\pi^{-1/2}(\zeta/h^{2/3})^{-1/4} e^{+\Sigma(\xi)}(1+O(h))$, because $	frac23\zeta^{3/2}=\Sigma(\xi)$ by the definition of $\zeta$ and [F1]; multiplying by $a(\zeta)=\zeta^{1/4}(W-\lambda)^{-1/4}$ (from $\zeta(\zeta')^{2}=W-\lambda$) turns these into the forbidden-side forms of (a) and (b) with the constants $C=	frac12\pi^{-1/2}h^{1/6}
e0$ and $C'=\pi^{-1/2}h^{1/6}
e0$, up to the displayed errors, which are absorbed into $\delta_{\mathrm{f}}$, $\delta_{\mathrm{f}}'$ with the bound of [F3].
